#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""The network modules: every program runs as the pinned compiler runs it.

TCP, UDP, HTTP and WebSocket (spec/alchem-network.md). Every program stays on
this machine: it speaks to 127.0.0.1 (or ::1) on ports the system chooses, to
port 1 where a refused connection is the point, and looks no name up but
localhost. Nothing printed holds a port, an address of the host, a time or a
process number. A program whose expected output has a "<timeout>" in it waits
for an answer that must not come; every answer that must come is waited for
without a clock that could cut it short.

SIX are the programs that need IPv6 on the loopback interface; they are
skipped, and said to be, where ::1 cannot be bound. OWN are programs only
the self-hosted compiler takes: they call the library's own functions (the
module is named AlchemProbe, which lets it) to put the name-server client
against a name server of the program's own on 127.0.0.1, since the client
otherwise asks the servers of /etc/resolv.conf.

Each native program runs under scripts/limited in a session of its own,
which is killed whole when the program is done or out of time: a server
that never stops is exactly what hangs."""
from concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor
from pathlib import Path
import os
import signal
import socket
import subprocess
import sys
